      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "AS-BUILT 2026-08-20 (S6, publish doors): ONE new CROSS-TENANT READ SURFACE, and the two whitelists\nthat bound it. No new collection, no new store, no change to any tenancy predicate.\n\nTHE REVIEW QUEUE. `GET /api/v1/integrations/definitions/publish-requests` (super-admin) lists every\n`org` row, ACROSS EVERY ORG, whose tenant has asked to publish it. It is the only response in the\nprocess that carries a tenant row to a platform super-admin who is not a member of that org, so:\n  - THE WINDOW IS OPENED FROM INSIDE THE TENANT. `isDefinitionVisibleTo`'s submit branch (E2) is\n    unchanged - a non-member super-admin sees an `org` row ONLY while `publishRequest` is present.\n    A `private` row is never in scope (submission requires `org`, re-checked at the read), and the\n    withdraw closes the window again. This is emphatically NOT a super-admin read exception; the\n    A1 model still keeps every tenant's private authoring invisible to every role.\n  - IT CARRIES NO CONTENT. `publishQueueEntry` is a whitelist: id, key, displayName, orgId,\n    actionCount, republish, requestedBy, requestedAt, note. No skillMd, no lessons, no\n    configSchema, no action bodies - because the queue runs NO scrub, and that content is exactly\n    what publish-scrub.ts exists to clean before it crosses an org boundary. A reviewer reads\n    content through POST .../publish-preview, which IS the scrub.\n  - THE ONE FREE-TEXT FIELD ON IT IS SCRUBBED AT THE WRITE. `publishRequest.note` had no writer\n    before this slice. It is now scrubbed through the PUBLISH floor (`scrubPublishText`) and capped\n    at 1000 characters at the route, before the store sees it.\n\nTHE PUBLISHED SNAPSHOT'S OWN TENANCY got stricter in the same slice: its actions are a whitelist\nrather than a spread, and the platform-authored `authoring` record is projected so no user id from\nthe authoring tenant (`authoredBy`, `trustedBy`) and no author prose (`goal`,\n`verification.checks[].detail`) crosses an org boundary. The trust semantics survive intact - see\n05-data-model, and note that KEEPING `state` is what stops a provisional action reading as\nhuman-written (and therefore trusted) in every consuming org.\n\nSuites: api/tests/security/publish-doors-isolation.test.ts (the D5 structural exclusion by key-set\nequality, the provenance projection, the note) plus the cross-org legs of\napi/tests/contract/integrations-publish.test.ts; the credential-containment half stays in\napi/tests/security/integration-publish-scrub.test.ts.",
      "rawText": "AS-BUILT 2026-08-20 (S6, publish doors): ONE new CROSS-TENANT READ SURFACE, and the two whitelists\nthat bound it. No new collection, no new store, no change to any tenancy predicate.\n\nTHE REVIEW QUEUE. `GET /api/v1/integrations/definitions/publish-requests` (super-admin) lists every\n`org` row, ACROSS EVERY ORG, whose tenant has asked to publish it. It is the only response in the\nprocess that carries a tenant row to a platform super-admin who is not a member of that org, so:\n  - THE WINDOW IS OPENED FROM INSIDE THE TENANT. `isDefinitionVisibleTo`'s submit branch (E2) is\n    unchanged - a non-member super-admin sees an `org` row ONLY while `publishRequest` is present.\n    A `private` row is never in scope (submission requires `org`, re-checked at the read), and the\n    withdraw closes the window again. This is emphatically NOT a super-admin read exception; the\n    A1 model still keeps every tenant's private authoring invisible to every role.\n  - IT CARRIES NO CONTENT. `publishQueueEntry` is a whitelist: id, key, displayName, orgId,\n    actionCount, republish, requestedBy, requestedAt, note. No skillMd, no lessons, no\n    configSchema, no action bodies - because the queue runs NO scrub, and that content is exactly\n    what publish-scrub.ts exists to clean before it crosses an org boundary. A reviewer reads\n    content through POST .../publish-preview, which IS the scrub.\n  - THE ONE FREE-TEXT FIELD ON IT IS SCRUBBED AT THE WRITE. `publishRequest.note` had no writer\n    before this slice. It is now scrubbed through the PUBLISH floor (`scrubPublishText`) and capped\n    at 1000 characters at the route, before the store sees it.\n\nTHE PUBLISHED SNAPSHOT'S OWN TENANCY got stricter in the same slice: its actions are a whitelist\nrather than a spread, and the platform-authored `authoring` record is projected so no user id from\nthe authoring tenant (`authoredBy`, `trustedBy`) and no author prose (`goal`,\n`verification.checks[].detail`) crosses an org boundary. The trust semantics survive intact - see\n05-data-model, and note that KEEPING `state` is what stops a provisional action reading as\nhuman-written (and therefore trusted) in every consuming org.\n\nSuites: api/tests/security/publish-doors-isolation.test.ts (the D5 structural exclusion by key-set\nequality, the provenance projection, the note) plus the cross-org legs of\napi/tests/contract/integrations-publish.test.ts; the credential-containment half stays in\napi/tests/security/integration-publish-scrub.test.ts.",
      "originalText": "AS-BUILT 2026-08-20 (S6, publish doors): ONE new CROSS-TENANT READ SURFACE, and the two whitelists\nthat bound it. No new collection, no new store, no change to any tenancy predicate.\n\nTHE REVIEW QUEUE. `GET /api/v1/integrations/definitions/publish-requests` (super-admin) lists every\n`org` row, ACROSS EVERY ORG, whose tenant has asked to publish it. It is the only response in the\nprocess that carries a tenant row to a platform super-admin who is not a member of that org, so:\n  - THE WINDOW IS OPENED FROM INSIDE THE TENANT. `isDefinitionVisibleTo`'s submit branch (E2) is\n    unchanged - a non-member super-admin sees an `org` row ONLY while `publishRequest` is present.\n    A `private` row is never in scope (submission requires `org`, re-checked at the read), and the\n    withdraw closes the window again. This is emphatically NOT a super-admin read exception; the\n    A1 model still keeps every tenant's private authoring invisible to every role.\n  - IT CARRIES NO CONTENT. `publishQueueEntry` is a whitelist: id, key, displayName, orgId,\n    actionCount, republish, requestedBy, requestedAt, note. No skillMd, no lessons, no\n    configSchema, no action bodies - because the queue runs NO scrub, and that content is exactly\n    what publish-scrub.ts exists to clean before it crosses an org boundary. A reviewer reads\n    content through POST .../publish-preview, which IS the scrub.\n  - THE ONE FREE-TEXT FIELD ON IT IS SCRUBBED AT THE WRITE. `publishRequest.note` had no writer\n    before this slice. It is now scrubbed through the PUBLISH floor (`scrubPublishText`) and capped\n    at 1000 characters at the route, before the store sees it.\n\nTHE PUBLISHED SNAPSHOT'S OWN TENANCY got stricter in the same slice: its actions are a whitelist\nrather than a spread, and the platform-authored `authoring` record is projected so no user id from\nthe authoring tenant (`authoredBy`, `trustedBy`) and no author prose (`goal`,\n`verification.checks[].detail`) crosses an org boundary. The trust semantics survive intact - see\n05-data-model, and note that KEEPING `state` is what stops a provisional action reading as\nhuman-written (and therefore trusted) in every consuming org.\n\nSuites: api/tests/security/publish-doors-isolation.test.ts (the D5 structural exclusion by key-set\nequality, the provenance projection, the note) plus the cross-org legs of\napi/tests/contract/integrations-publish.test.ts; the credential-containment half stays in\napi/tests/security/integration-publish-scrub.test.ts.",
