      "locked": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "text": "AS-BUILT 2026-08-20 (S6, correction): THE REVIEW QUEUE NOW RUNS THE PUBLISH FLOOR. The annotation\nabove says \"IT CARRIES NO CONTENT ... because the queue runs NO scrub\". The first half was FALSE for\ntwo of the nine fields on that whitelist, so the second half was a hole rather than a design.\n\nWHAT WAS WRONG. `key` and `displayName` are PACKAGE FIELDS. `packageConfigFromDoc` puts both in the\npublished config and `applyPublishFloor` walks both, so a definition named `CRM sk_live_<key>`\npublished as `CRM [REDACTED]` - while `publishQueueEntry` returned `doc.displayName` verbatim to a\nsuper-admin of ANOTHER ORG. The queue was therefore a strictly WIDER read of tenant content than the\npreview it is documented as being narrower than: the surface that needs no per-row admission had the\nlaxer scrub. Proven live, not reasoned: an org-A row so named was served to org B's super-admin as\nthe literal string while its own published snapshot read `CRM [REDACTED]`.\n\nAS BUILT NOW. `publishQueueEntry` reads its content fields off `applyPublishFloor(...).content.config`\nrather than off the document - the SAME floor the preview and the publish run, not a second narrower\nrule. A field added to `IntegrationPublishQueueEntry` later is scrubbed by WHERE IT IS READ FROM.\nThe note is scrubbed here too, which is a different property from the submit route's scrub and not a\nrepeat of it: the route scrubs so a credential never reaches the DATABASE, this scrubs so nothing on\nthe row reaches ANOTHER ORG however it got stored (`requestPublish` takes the string it is given).\n\nIDENTITY IS STILL CARRIED, DELIBERATELY. `orgId` and `requestedBy` are the queue's reason to exist -\na reviewer handing a package to every organisation must know whose it is - and they are exactly what\nthe PUBLISHED artifact withholds (`publishableAuthoringOf` drops `authoredBy`/`trustedBy`). The two\nsurfaces differ there on purpose and in opposite directions: publication is anonymous and permanent,\nreview is attributed and revocable.\n\nTHE NOTE'S CAP MOVED to `requestPublish` (the one place the note is written), so it bounds the field\nrather than one caller. The route keeps the content scrub only; `definition-store.ts` still holds no\nruntime dependency on the modules that own a scrub, and a length is not a content rule.\n\nRESIDUAL, STATED: a secret-shaped `key` still crosses org boundaries raw on the PUBLISHED read path.\n`publishedViewOf` restores `key: doc.key` over the snapshot on purpose (a snapshot never renames the\nrow, and the registry resolves by key), so it cannot be scrubbed there without breaking resolution.\nThe queue is now narrower than publish for that field, which is the safe direction. docs/findings.md.\n\nSuites: the queue legs of api/tests/security/publish-doors-isolation.test.ts - the display name\ncompared BYTE-FOR-BYTE against the publish floor's answer for the same row, a note written straight\nthrough the store and scrubbed on the way out, and the store's own super-admin filter called\ndirectly because `requireRole` in front of it makes that layer invisible from the wire.",
      "rawText": "AS-BUILT 2026-08-20 (S6, correction): THE REVIEW QUEUE NOW RUNS THE PUBLISH FLOOR. The annotation\nabove says \"IT CARRIES NO CONTENT ... because the queue runs NO scrub\". The first half was FALSE for\ntwo of the nine fields on that whitelist, so the second half was a hole rather than a design.\n\nWHAT WAS WRONG. `key` and `displayName` are PACKAGE FIELDS. `packageConfigFromDoc` puts both in the\npublished config and `applyPublishFloor` walks both, so a definition named `CRM sk_live_<key>`\npublished as `CRM [REDACTED]` - while `publishQueueEntry` returned `doc.displayName` verbatim to a\nsuper-admin of ANOTHER ORG. The queue was therefore a strictly WIDER read of tenant content than the\npreview it is documented as being narrower than: the surface that needs no per-row admission had the\nlaxer scrub. Proven live, not reasoned: an org-A row so named was served to org B's super-admin as\nthe literal string while its own published snapshot read `CRM [REDACTED]`.\n\nAS BUILT NOW. `publishQueueEntry` reads its content fields off `applyPublishFloor(...).content.config`\nrather than off the document - the SAME floor the preview and the publish run, not a second narrower\nrule. A field added to `IntegrationPublishQueueEntry` later is scrubbed by WHERE IT IS READ FROM.\nThe note is scrubbed here too, which is a different property from the submit route's scrub and not a\nrepeat of it: the route scrubs so a credential never reaches the DATABASE, this scrubs so nothing on\nthe row reaches ANOTHER ORG however it got stored (`requestPublish` takes the string it is given).\n\nIDENTITY IS STILL CARRIED, DELIBERATELY. `orgId` and `requestedBy` are the queue's reason to exist -\na reviewer handing a package to every organisation must know whose it is - and they are exactly what\nthe PUBLISHED artifact withholds (`publishableAuthoringOf` drops `authoredBy`/`trustedBy`). The two\nsurfaces differ there on purpose and in opposite directions: publication is anonymous and permanent,\nreview is attributed and revocable.\n\nTHE NOTE'S CAP MOVED to `requestPublish` (the one place the note is written), so it bounds the field\nrather than one caller. The route keeps the content scrub only; `definition-store.ts` still holds no\nruntime dependency on the modules that own a scrub, and a length is not a content rule.\n\nRESIDUAL, STATED: a secret-shaped `key` still crosses org boundaries raw on the PUBLISHED read path.\n`publishedViewOf` restores `key: doc.key` over the snapshot on purpose (a snapshot never renames the\nrow, and the registry resolves by key), so it cannot be scrubbed there without breaking resolution.\nThe queue is now narrower than publish for that field, which is the safe direction. docs/findings.md.\n\nSuites: the queue legs of api/tests/security/publish-doors-isolation.test.ts - the display name\ncompared BYTE-FOR-BYTE against the publish floor's answer for the same row, a note written straight\nthrough the store and scrubbed on the way out, and the store's own super-admin filter called\ndirectly because `requireRole` in front of it makes that layer invisible from the wire.",
      "originalText": "AS-BUILT 2026-08-20 (S6, correction): THE REVIEW QUEUE NOW RUNS THE PUBLISH FLOOR. The annotation\nabove says \"IT CARRIES NO CONTENT ... because the queue runs NO scrub\". The first half was FALSE for\ntwo of the nine fields on that whitelist, so the second half was a hole rather than a design.\n\nWHAT WAS WRONG. `key` and `displayName` are PACKAGE FIELDS. `packageConfigFromDoc` puts both in the\npublished config and `applyPublishFloor` walks both, so a definition named `CRM sk_live_<key>`\npublished as `CRM [REDACTED]` - while `publishQueueEntry` returned `doc.displayName` verbatim to a\nsuper-admin of ANOTHER ORG. The queue was therefore a strictly WIDER read of tenant content than the\npreview it is documented as being narrower than: the surface that needs no per-row admission had the\nlaxer scrub. Proven live, not reasoned: an org-A row so named was served to org B's super-admin as\nthe literal string while its own published snapshot read `CRM [REDACTED]`.\n\nAS BUILT NOW. `publishQueueEntry` reads its content fields off `applyPublishFloor(...).content.config`\nrather than off the document - the SAME floor the preview and the publish run, not a second narrower\nrule. A field added to `IntegrationPublishQueueEntry` later is scrubbed by WHERE IT IS READ FROM.\nThe note is scrubbed here too, which is a different property from the submit route's scrub and not a\nrepeat of it: the route scrubs so a credential never reaches the DATABASE, this scrubs so nothing on\nthe row reaches ANOTHER ORG however it got stored (`requestPublish` takes the string it is given).\n\nIDENTITY IS STILL CARRIED, DELIBERATELY. `orgId` and `requestedBy` are the queue's reason to exist -\na reviewer handing a package to every organisation must know whose it is - and they are exactly what\nthe PUBLISHED artifact withholds (`publishableAuthoringOf` drops `authoredBy`/`trustedBy`). The two\nsurfaces differ there on purpose and in opposite directions: publication is anonymous and permanent,\nreview is attributed and revocable.\n\nTHE NOTE'S CAP MOVED to `requestPublish` (the one place the note is written), so it bounds the field\nrather than one caller. The route keeps the content scrub only; `definition-store.ts` still holds no\nruntime dependency on the modules that own a scrub, and a length is not a content rule.\n\nRESIDUAL, STATED: a secret-shaped `key` still crosses org boundaries raw on the PUBLISHED read path.\n`publishedViewOf` restores `key: doc.key` over the snapshot on purpose (a snapshot never renames the\nrow, and the registry resolves by key), so it cannot be scrubbed there without breaking resolution.\nThe queue is now narrower than publish for that field, which is the safe direction. docs/findings.md.\n\nSuites: the queue legs of api/tests/security/publish-doors-isolation.test.ts - the display name\ncompared BYTE-FOR-BYTE against the publish floor's answer for the same row, a note written straight\nthrough the store and scrubbed on the way out, and the store's own super-admin filter called\ndirectly because `requireRole` in front of it makes that layer invisible from the wire."
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "id": "asbuilt-20260820k-s6-one-door-across-the-boundary",
      "x": 60,
      "y": 3020,
      "width": 860.0,
      "height": 812.0,
      "fontSize": 14,
      "fontFamily": 3,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "strokeColor": "#b91c1c",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 1,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 0,
      "opacity": 100,
      "angle": 0,
      "seed": 553119042,
      "version": 2,
