      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "text": "AS-BUILT 2026-08-20 (S6 round 3, correction): THE REVIEW WINDOW IS A READ, AND THE `global` TIER\nNOW HAS EXACTLY ONE WRITER. Two corrections to what the blocks above imply, both about doors OTHER\nthan the five S6 mounted - and both reachable only BECAUSE S6 mounted them.\n\n1. THE WINDOW WIDENED A WRITE, NOT ONLY A READ (fixed). `isDefinitionVisibleTo` lets a super-admin\n   outside the authoring org SEE an `org` row while that org is asking to publish it. But\n   `canWriteDefinition` grants a super-admin WRITE over anything they can SEE - so opening the\n   window opened every write gated on that predicate alone, and `requestPublish` was one.\n   A super-admin of org B could RE-STAMP org A's request: `requestedBy` became theirs, and the note\n   arguing for publication became their words. Silently, since re-submission is idempotent by\n   design. `withdrawPublishRequest` already guarded on membership; the SUBMIT door did not, and the\n   asymmetry was the defect. Both doors now share ONE named term, `canWriteOwnPublishRequest`\n   (writability AND `sameOrg`), applied in the pre-check AND the F4b in-mutator re-assert.\n   THE RULE ON THIS DIAGRAM, restated: the window admits a non-member reviewer to READ (the queue,\n   the preview) and to decide publication. It admits them to NOTHING the tenant owns.\n\n2. THE OLD `POST /definitions/:id/global` WAS A SECOND, UNSCRUBBED CROSSING (fixed). It called\n   `setVisibility(..., 'global')`, so a row reached the cross-org tier with NO `publishedSnapshot`\n   and `publishedViewOf` served every other org the author's LIVE row through the read-time FLOOR:\n   no chokepoint model pass (the floor is layer 1 of 2), no `scrubbedBy`/`scrubbedAt`/`scrubVersion`,\n   and a broken lineage - `republish` is `publishedSnapshot !== undefined`, so the NEXT reviewer is\n   told a re-publication is a first one.\n   HOW S6 WORSENED IT: before the submit door existed nothing wrote `publishRequest`, so the window\n   branch was DEAD and this route answered 404 for a foreign tenant's `org` row. Mounting the submit\n   door handed the unscrubbed door its first foreign rows.\n   DECIDED - ONE WAY ACROSS THE BOUNDARY: `{global:true}` now calls `publishDefinition`, the same\n   function `POST .../publish` calls. Not two gated doors, because both landed on the SAME\n   `visibilityWriteVerdict(row, actor, 'global')` - identical admission, so one was a bypass of the\n   other, not an alternative to it. `{global:false}` is why the route survives: un-publishing has no\n   equivalent on the publish door, writes no snapshot (it creates no cross-org reader) and keeps the\n   existing one so the transition stays exactly reversible.\n\nNET EFFECT ON THIS DIAGRAM: no new collection, no new store, no new tenancy predicate and no wire\nshape change. What changed is the count of writers into the `global` tier - two, now one - and the\nmembership term on the publish-request field. The blocks above stay as written: append-only means\nthe record of what was believed survives its correction.",
      "rawText": "AS-BUILT 2026-08-20 (S6 round 3, correction): THE REVIEW WINDOW IS A READ, AND THE `global` TIER\nNOW HAS EXACTLY ONE WRITER. Two corrections to what the blocks above imply, both about doors OTHER\nthan the five S6 mounted - and both reachable only BECAUSE S6 mounted them.\n\n1. THE WINDOW WIDENED A WRITE, NOT ONLY A READ (fixed). `isDefinitionVisibleTo` lets a super-admin\n   outside the authoring org SEE an `org` row while that org is asking to publish it. But\n   `canWriteDefinition` grants a super-admin WRITE over anything they can SEE - so opening the\n   window opened every write gated on that predicate alone, and `requestPublish` was one.\n   A super-admin of org B could RE-STAMP org A's request: `requestedBy` became theirs, and the note\n   arguing for publication became their words. Silently, since re-submission is idempotent by\n   design. `withdrawPublishRequest` already guarded on membership; the SUBMIT door did not, and the\n   asymmetry was the defect. Both doors now share ONE named term, `canWriteOwnPublishRequest`\n   (writability AND `sameOrg`), applied in the pre-check AND the F4b in-mutator re-assert.\n   THE RULE ON THIS DIAGRAM, restated: the window admits a non-member reviewer to READ (the queue,\n   the preview) and to decide publication. It admits them to NOTHING the tenant owns.\n\n2. THE OLD `POST /definitions/:id/global` WAS A SECOND, UNSCRUBBED CROSSING (fixed). It called\n   `setVisibility(..., 'global')`, so a row reached the cross-org tier with NO `publishedSnapshot`\n   and `publishedViewOf` served every other org the author's LIVE row through the read-time FLOOR:\n   no chokepoint model pass (the floor is layer 1 of 2), no `scrubbedBy`/`scrubbedAt`/`scrubVersion`,\n   and a broken lineage - `republish` is `publishedSnapshot !== undefined`, so the NEXT reviewer is\n   told a re-publication is a first one.\n   HOW S6 WORSENED IT: before the submit door existed nothing wrote `publishRequest`, so the window\n   branch was DEAD and this route answered 404 for a foreign tenant's `org` row. Mounting the submit\n   door handed the unscrubbed door its first foreign rows.\n   DECIDED - ONE WAY ACROSS THE BOUNDARY: `{global:true}` now calls `publishDefinition`, the same\n   function `POST .../publish` calls. Not two gated doors, because both landed on the SAME\n   `visibilityWriteVerdict(row, actor, 'global')` - identical admission, so one was a bypass of the\n   other, not an alternative to it. `{global:false}` is why the route survives: un-publishing has no\n   equivalent on the publish door, writes no snapshot (it creates no cross-org reader) and keeps the\n   existing one so the transition stays exactly reversible.\n\nNET EFFECT ON THIS DIAGRAM: no new collection, no new store, no new tenancy predicate and no wire\nshape change. What changed is the count of writers into the `global` tier - two, now one - and the\nmembership term on the publish-request field. The blocks above stay as written: append-only means\nthe record of what was believed survives its correction.",
      "originalText": "AS-BUILT 2026-08-20 (S6 round 3, correction): THE REVIEW WINDOW IS A READ, AND THE `global` TIER\nNOW HAS EXACTLY ONE WRITER. Two corrections to what the blocks above imply, both about doors OTHER\nthan the five S6 mounted - and both reachable only BECAUSE S6 mounted them.\n\n1. THE WINDOW WIDENED A WRITE, NOT ONLY A READ (fixed). `isDefinitionVisibleTo` lets a super-admin\n   outside the authoring org SEE an `org` row while that org is asking to publish it. But\n   `canWriteDefinition` grants a super-admin WRITE over anything they can SEE - so opening the\n   window opened every write gated on that predicate alone, and `requestPublish` was one.\n   A super-admin of org B could RE-STAMP org A's request: `requestedBy` became theirs, and the note\n   arguing for publication became their words. Silently, since re-submission is idempotent by\n   design. `withdrawPublishRequest` already guarded on membership; the SUBMIT door did not, and the\n   asymmetry was the defect. Both doors now share ONE named term, `canWriteOwnPublishRequest`\n   (writability AND `sameOrg`), applied in the pre-check AND the F4b in-mutator re-assert.\n   THE RULE ON THIS DIAGRAM, restated: the window admits a non-member reviewer to READ (the queue,\n   the preview) and to decide publication. It admits them to NOTHING the tenant owns.\n\n2. THE OLD `POST /definitions/:id/global` WAS A SECOND, UNSCRUBBED CROSSING (fixed). It called\n   `setVisibility(..., 'global')`, so a row reached the cross-org tier with NO `publishedSnapshot`\n   and `publishedViewOf` served every other org the author's LIVE row through the read-time FLOOR:\n   no chokepoint model pass (the floor is layer 1 of 2), no `scrubbedBy`/`scrubbedAt`/`scrubVersion`,\n   and a broken lineage - `republish` is `publishedSnapshot !== undefined`, so the NEXT reviewer is\n   told a re-publication is a first one.\n   HOW S6 WORSENED IT: before the submit door existed nothing wrote `publishRequest`, so the window\n   branch was DEAD and this route answered 404 for a foreign tenant's `org` row. Mounting the submit\n   door handed the unscrubbed door its first foreign rows.\n   DECIDED - ONE WAY ACROSS THE BOUNDARY: `{global:true}` now calls `publishDefinition`, the same\n   function `POST .../publish` calls. Not two gated doors, because both landed on the SAME\n   `visibilityWriteVerdict(row, actor, 'global')` - identical admission, so one was a bypass of the\n   other, not an alternative to it. `{global:false}` is why the route survives: un-publishing has no\n   equivalent on the publish door, writes no snapshot (it creates no cross-org reader) and keeps the\n   existing one so the transition stays exactly reversible.\n\nNET EFFECT ON THIS DIAGRAM: no new collection, no new store, no new tenancy predicate and no wire\nshape change. What changed is the count of writers into the `global` tier - two, now one - and the\nmembership term on the publish-request field. The blocks above stay as written: append-only means\nthe record of what was believed survives its correction."
    },
