      "locked": false,
      "text": "AS-BUILT 2026-08-20 (S6 round 4, correction): A PROMOTION IS IDEMPOTENT; A RE-PUBLISH IS THE OTHER\nDOOR. The block above records that `POST /definitions/:id/global` {global:true} became an ALIAS of\n`publishDefinition`, so the `global` tier has exactly one writer. True, and it left one edge on this\nflow unstated. `visibilityWriteVerdict` permits global -> global ON PURPOSE (E2 narrowed the\nlaunch-pad rule to `=== private` so a published artifact can be refreshed at all), so after the fold\na SECOND {global:true} on the same row was no longer a no-op: it re-scrubbed the author's CURRENT\nlive row, replaced the frozen artifact every consuming org reads, and stamped `supersedes` - with no\npreview and no reviewer in the loop. A retry after a timeout is enough to fire it.\n\nWHAT THE FLOW IS NOW. The `/global` door asks for a PROMOTION (`promoteOnly`), so:\n\n  row is `org` + submitted ........ scrub -> snapshot -> tier `global`   (the promotion, unchanged)\n  row is `global` WITH a snapshot .. NOTHING IS WRITTEN; answers the tier  (`already-published`)\n  row is `global` with NO snapshot . scrub -> snapshot                   (repairs the legacy state)\n  {global:false} ................... tier -> `org`, artifact KEPT        (unchanged)\n\nThe third arm is why this is not simply \"refuse a second call\": a `global` row with no snapshot is\nthe exact state the fold exists to end (a legacy-runtime import, or a row flipped by the pre-fold\nroute), and it is served cross-org through the READ-TIME FLOOR until something publishes it.\n\nA DELIBERATE RE-PUBLISH IS `POST /definitions/:id/publish`, whose response IS the snapshot stamp and\nwhose caller has just read a preview. The supersede edge on this diagram belongs to THAT door only.\n\nAND THE TIER INVARIANT IS ROUTE-SCOPED, stated here because a diagram is where an over-wide claim\ndoes the most damage. \"Nothing reaches the `global` tier without an artifact\" holds over the MOUNTED\nHTTP routes. `legacy-runtime-import.ts` and a direct store `create({visibility:'global'})` are NOT on\nthis flow and are not covered; what makes them safe is the read-path floor, not this invariant.",
      "rawText": "AS-BUILT 2026-08-20 (S6 round 4, correction): A PROMOTION IS IDEMPOTENT; A RE-PUBLISH IS THE OTHER\nDOOR. The block above records that `POST /definitions/:id/global` {global:true} became an ALIAS of\n`publishDefinition`, so the `global` tier has exactly one writer. True, and it left one edge on this\nflow unstated. `visibilityWriteVerdict` permits global -> global ON PURPOSE (E2 narrowed the\nlaunch-pad rule to `=== private` so a published artifact can be refreshed at all), so after the fold\na SECOND {global:true} on the same row was no longer a no-op: it re-scrubbed the author's CURRENT\nlive row, replaced the frozen artifact every consuming org reads, and stamped `supersedes` - with no\npreview and no reviewer in the loop. A retry after a timeout is enough to fire it.\n\nWHAT THE FLOW IS NOW. The `/global` door asks for a PROMOTION (`promoteOnly`), so:\n\n  row is `org` + submitted ........ scrub -> snapshot -> tier `global`   (the promotion, unchanged)\n  row is `global` WITH a snapshot .. NOTHING IS WRITTEN; answers the tier  (`already-published`)\n  row is `global` with NO snapshot . scrub -> snapshot                   (repairs the legacy state)\n  {global:false} ................... tier -> `org`, artifact KEPT        (unchanged)\n\nThe third arm is why this is not simply \"refuse a second call\": a `global` row with no snapshot is\nthe exact state the fold exists to end (a legacy-runtime import, or a row flipped by the pre-fold\nroute), and it is served cross-org through the READ-TIME FLOOR until something publishes it.\n\nA DELIBERATE RE-PUBLISH IS `POST /definitions/:id/publish`, whose response IS the snapshot stamp and\nwhose caller has just read a preview. The supersede edge on this diagram belongs to THAT door only.\n\nAND THE TIER INVARIANT IS ROUTE-SCOPED, stated here because a diagram is where an over-wide claim\ndoes the most damage. \"Nothing reaches the `global` tier without an artifact\" holds over the MOUNTED\nHTTP routes. `legacy-runtime-import.ts` and a direct store `create({visibility:'global'})` are NOT on\nthis flow and are not covered; what makes them safe is the read-path floor, not this invariant.",
      "originalText": "AS-BUILT 2026-08-20 (S6 round 4, correction): A PROMOTION IS IDEMPOTENT; A RE-PUBLISH IS THE OTHER\nDOOR. The block above records that `POST /definitions/:id/global` {global:true} became an ALIAS of\n`publishDefinition`, so the `global` tier has exactly one writer. True, and it left one edge on this\nflow unstated. `visibilityWriteVerdict` permits global -> global ON PURPOSE (E2 narrowed the\nlaunch-pad rule to `=== private` so a published artifact can be refreshed at all), so after the fold\na SECOND {global:true} on the same row was no longer a no-op: it re-scrubbed the author's CURRENT\nlive row, replaced the frozen artifact every consuming org reads, and stamped `supersedes` - with no\npreview and no reviewer in the loop. A retry after a timeout is enough to fire it.\n\nWHAT THE FLOW IS NOW. The `/global` door asks for a PROMOTION (`promoteOnly`), so:\n\n  row is `org` + submitted ........ scrub -> snapshot -> tier `global`   (the promotion, unchanged)\n  row is `global` WITH a snapshot .. NOTHING IS WRITTEN; answers the tier  (`already-published`)\n  row is `global` with NO snapshot . scrub -> snapshot                   (repairs the legacy state)\n  {global:false} ................... tier -> `org`, artifact KEPT        (unchanged)\n\nThe third arm is why this is not simply \"refuse a second call\": a `global` row with no snapshot is\nthe exact state the fold exists to end (a legacy-runtime import, or a row flipped by the pre-fold\nroute), and it is served cross-org through the READ-TIME FLOOR until something publishes it.\n\nA DELIBERATE RE-PUBLISH IS `POST /definitions/:id/publish`, whose response IS the snapshot stamp and\nwhose caller has just read a preview. The supersede edge on this diagram belongs to THAT door only.\n\nAND THE TIER INVARIANT IS ROUTE-SCOPED, stated here because a diagram is where an over-wide claim\ndoes the most damage. \"Nothing reaches the `global` tier without an artifact\" holds over the MOUNTED\nHTTP routes. `legacy-runtime-import.ts` and a direct store `create({visibility:'global'})` are NOT on\nthis flow and are not covered; what makes them safe is the read-path floor, not this invariant.",
